    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d149">

                <pb facs="0297" n="213" xml:id="pg213"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860C.01/11–2644</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stettinius</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        President </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-11-28">November 28, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum for the President</hi>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Resignation of Mikolajczyk </p>
                <p>I think you will be interested in looking at the two enclosed telegrams in regard
                    to the Polish situation, the first reporting a conversation between Mikolajczyk
                    and Schoenfeld in which the former explained in some detail his reasons for
                        resigning<anchor n="1" xml:id="d149-f1"/> and the latter quoting extensively
                    from British press discussion of the subject.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d149-f2"/>
                    You will notice that a number of British papers erroneously attribute
                    Mikolajczyk’s resignation to our refusal to guarantee Polish frontiers.</p>
                <p>In order to counteract the London news stories regarding the frontier guarantee,
                    the Department on November 25 released the following statement:</p>
                <p>The specific question of the guarantee of the Polish frontier by this Government
                    was not and could not have been an issue since this Government’s traditional
                    policy of not guaranteeing specific frontiers in Europe is well known.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward</hi> R. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Stettinius, Jr</hi>. </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
